			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2093982">
									

<p>You know, I didn't even consider the similarities between this and your SCP-1932, but I can kind of see it now that you mention it. And while I definitely remembered your SCP because it was posted minutes before I posted SCP-1562, I can promise that any similarities are unintentional. I know you're not making an accusation, just wanted to acknowledge what you are saying.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Look at the lethality this way: The &quot;A&quot; seat functions exactly as intended. It bends reality for just the user, to provide a little extra fun, though it's certainly questionable if children would enjoy that. The &quot;Ø&quot; seat is defective/broken; so it will function properly if the rider on the &quot;D&quot; seat pushes off at the proper moment. Its effects only become extreme after the &quot;D&quot; rider stays in the ground position for too long. Any deaths were brought about due to misuse of a broken object, and not because of any malicious intent by its creator, or hokey attempts at making it grimdark by me. The other world isn't some wacky dimension, it's a broken reality from a defective plaything, that only affects the rider of that seat. Like my other 2 playground SCPs, it's a well-intentioned failure created by&#8230;a well-intentioned failure.</p>
</div>
